I disagree. I will often pray for a full belly when I have food in my
pack, especially when playing a wizard where food (un)availablility can
make the early game really really hard.

Why? Because if you eat the food, and then after a while get hungry
again, your only choice is to pray. But then you will inevitably get
hungry again, and if your prayer timeout hasn't expired from that last
prayer you are well and truly shafted.

However, if you pray first time, keeping the food in reserve, then when
you get hungry again and you think maybe your prayer timeout hasn't
reset yet you still have a way to stay alive until you can safely pray
for the second time.

Aslo, if there's an altar about then I'll often preserve food stocks
and let myself get weak because I'm about to pray for water anyway.
There's nothing to stop you getting holy water AND a full belly from
the same prayer, and in the early game you have to squeeze as much as
you can out of everything.

Question is, should the Gods look at your inventory when considering
how desperate your hunger is? I suppose all players would do in that
case is dump their food, walk away two steps and *then* pray. Might be
a nice little thought for a deity-differentiation patch though.

Having said all that, in the OP's case with 9 food rations in his
pocket, I would definitely reserve the prayer for other emergencies,
(especially since he's trying to keep his XP down and hence is likely
to need HP-prayers) eat one of the food rations and use the rest and/or
heaps of gold for credit cloning.



